# Getdown Services announce ‘The Massive Champion Ship’ curated UK Summer 2027 shows

> 🎵 *Curated Music Story from NME*

Getdown Services perform live in 2026. CREDIT: James Klug/Getty Images

Getdown Services have announced a series of ‘The Massive Champion Ship’ curated live shows for the UK next summer, and dropped a new music video for ‘Probiotic’.

The Bristol duo released their critically-acclaimed second album ‘Massive Champion’ this summer, as part of a packed year that included a viral slot on Later… with Jools Holland, a gig as part of Harry Styles’ Meltdown Festival at Southbank Centre, and headline shows across Europe, the US and Australia. Now Josh Law and Ben Sadler have announced a run of dates around their appearance at Glastonbury 2027.

The ‘Massive Champion Ship’ tour kicks off at Glasgow’s Kelvingrove Bandstand on June 4, before continuing with a show in Blackpool and three back to back nights at London’s Troxy later that month. Dates wrap up at The Minack Theatre in Cornwall on June 25, and the band have promised to deliver “an extravaganza bonanza experience, with unconventional line-ups, fun surprises, some crushing lows, but mainly loads of fat smiles.”

“Think The Little Mermaid meets The Greatest Showman meets Alien: Romulus,” they added.

Tickets go on sale this Friday (November 27) at 10am GMT, and pre-sale options are open now. Visit here for tickets and more information.

JUNE 20274 – Kelvingrove Bandstand, Glasgow12 – Blackpool Tower Ballroom, Blackpool17 – Troxy, London18 – Troxy, London19 – Troxy, London25 – The Minack Theatre, Cornwall

As well as announcing new tour dates, today (Wednesday November 25) also sees Getdown Services release a new music video for ‘Massive Champion’ song ‘Probiotic’.

The video for the riff-heavy song in keeping with the lyrics – showing Sadler gradually feeling the strange effects after drinking a strange drink “with bits in it”. Check it out below.

The upcoming UK dates will take place shortly before the former NME Cover stars head over to Berlin in July to support Harry Styles on the final shows of his huge ‘Together, Together’ tour.

‘Massive Champion’ was given a glowing four star review from NME, and praised as an “unlikely triumph of heart and humour”.

‘Massive Champion’ stands triumphant as a record, whether they’ve shaken off their lifelong hang-ups or not,” it read. “Rather than let famous fans or the serotonin-inducing hedonism of their live shows push their sound into all-out silliness, the duo have honed their increased confidence into something more focussed and controlled, with nuance and left turns aplenty to match the daftness.”

